Right. And like half of them have the wrong home address.

3
00:00:09,180 --> 00:00:11,260
Exactly. It's so common.

4
00:00:11,920 --> 00:00:15,980
Today on the Deep Dive, we're looking at the GovStack digital registry specification.

5
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:19,040
Our mission here is to figure out how governments can, um,

6
00:00:19,660 --> 00:00:22,200
stop hoarding duplicated, fragmented data.

7
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:28,580
Yeah, and start sharing it safely without, you know, accidentally building some terrifying master database.

8
00:00:29,040 --> 00:00:33,760
Because right now, if you're running, say, the scholarship office and the school feeding program,

9
00:00:34,420 --> 00:00:37,780
and everyone just gets their own standalone copy of the data...

10
00:00:38,300 --> 00:00:41,940
You just end up with a dozen lists that are siloed and immediately go out of date.

11
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:50,380
Right. So the solution in the GovStack specs is having every office call a single register through a, uh, strictly published interface.

12
00:00:50,660 --> 00:00:58,620
Exactly. But wait, if everything is pulling from one central register, doesn't that just create this massive single point of failure?

13
00:00:58,720 --> 00:01:03,900
Like, how do you build that without exposing the entire database to anyone who asks?

14
00:01:04,379 --> 00:01:06,320
Well, that's where the reference architecture comes in.

15
00:01:06,940 --> 00:01:12,240
It establishes this really hard rule where outside systems, even other government departments,

16
00:01:12,840 --> 00:01:15,140
they never actually reach the registry directly.

17
00:01:15,800 --> 00:01:17,960
Oh, okay. So they go through a middleman?

18
00:01:18,460 --> 00:01:25,980
Basically, yeah. They pass through specific building blocks, like a registration block, and they get routed through an information mediator.

19
00:01:26,900 --> 00:01:30,820
Information mediator. That sounds, I don't know, incredibly vague.

20
00:01:31,660 --> 00:01:33,920
How do these systems actually talk to it securely?

21
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:40,220
So they use these highly specific generated services that are explicitly described using OpenAPI.

22
00:01:41,080 --> 00:01:44,020
Think of OpenAPI like a, um, a strict

23
00:01:44,640 --> 00:01:46,200
standardized menu for software.

24
00:01:46,840 --> 00:01:56,440
Okay. The requesting department knows exactly what data they can order, and the registry creates dedicated services just for, you know, creating, reading, or updating those exact

25
00:01:56,480 --> 00:01:56,880
records.

26
00:01:57,620 --> 00:02:01,640
So you can't just order off menu or say, "Hey, give me everything you've got."

27
00:02:01,940 --> 00:02:02,400
Exactly.

28
00:02:03,140 --> 00:02:10,860
You can't. And notably, while the spec mentions things like bulk operations and event subscriptions, those operations aren't published yet.

29
00:02:11,620 --> 00:02:15,120
Wait, why hide the bulk operations if the system can technically handle them?

30
00:02:15,500 --> 00:02:22,140
Because if you allow a department to download a million records at once, it completely defeats the purpose of granular security.

31
00:02:22,660 --> 00:02:23,900
Oh, right. That makes total sense.

32
00:02:24,180 --> 00:02:25,180
It's a huge data risk.

33
00:02:25,380 --> 00:02:27,640
Yeah. It just duplicates the data all over again.

34
00:02:28,060 --> 00:02:29,620
Let's ground this with an example. Mm-hmm.

35
00:02:29,620 --> 00:02:38,140
So in the fictional country of Progressa from the material, if their school feeding program wants to check a student's record through this mediator, who is actually guarding the door?

36
00:02:38,340 --> 00:02:39,620
Is it like a bouncer at a club?

37
00:02:40,200 --> 00:02:44,100
Not exactly. A bouncer just checks your ID and lets you into the whole club, right?

38
00:02:45,060 --> 00:02:49,560
This system, which in fictional Progressa uses a data exchange layer called Linkup,

39
00:02:50,320 --> 00:02:52,300
it acts a lot more like a bank teller.

40
00:02:52,840 --> 00:02:53,940
A bank teller. Okay, how so?

41
00:02:54,340 --> 00:02:56,100
Well, you can't just walk into the bank vault.

42
00:02:56,540 --> 00:03:03,720
You hand the teller your slip, they check your authorization, go to the vault, and bring back only the specific twenty dollar bill you asked for.

43
00:03:04,480 --> 00:03:13,100
Ah. So the consumer, like the feeding program, has to ask for the service, but the provider, the learner registry, is the teller deciding if they actually get that data.

44
00:03:13,480 --> 00:03:16,300
Precisely. The provider is always the one in control.

45
00:03:16,920 --> 00:03:19,080
They decide who is allowed to call their services.

46
00:03:19,700 --> 00:03:21,660
So once the teller approves your slip,

47
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:25,040
what does that access actually look like inside the system?

48
00:03:25,580 --> 00:03:32,240
It is highly, highly restricted. We're talking granular control here, per service, per record, and per field.

49
00:03:32,560 --> 00:03:35,080
Oh, wow. Down to the individual field?

50
00:03:35,420 --> 00:03:35,700
Yes.

51
00:03:36,320 --> 00:03:40,900
So in Progressa, the registration service can create and update learner records,

52
00:03:41,540 --> 00:03:42,460
but every other service,

53
00:03:43,120 --> 00:03:43,920
strictly read only.

54
00:03:44,260 --> 00:03:46,880
And they don't even get to read the whole file, do they?

55
00:03:47,300 --> 00:03:47,580
Nope.

56
00:03:48,200 --> 00:03:51,580
They only see the specific fields they absolutely need to do their jobs.

57
00:03:52,460 --> 00:04:01,660
That's huge because if the IT rules dictate exactly who sees what field, then the IT rules and the government data policy rules are literally the same document.

58
00:04:02,040 --> 00:04:09,700
Exactly. That eliminates all those classic translation errors between the developers building the system and the politicians writing the laws.

59
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:13,400
Yeah. A policy decision means the exact same thing in both rooms.

60
00:04:14,700 --> 00:04:20,060
And that shared language builds the foundation for really the most critical part of this architecture, which is

61
00:04:20,700 --> 00:04:22,500
trust. Trust. Right.

62
00:04:23,140 --> 00:04:26,200
Because every single time personal data is read, it's logged.

63
00:04:26,860 --> 00:04:31,800
The system records which record, which field, who read it, and exactly when.

64
00:04:32,480 --> 00:04:35,280
But, you know, it's easy to promise transparency in a policy document.

65
00:04:35,700 --> 00:04:38,720
How does the system actually prove it works before it goes live?

66
00:04:38,760 --> 00:04:44,620
Like, if a read-only service suddenly tries to rewrite a student's file, what happens in testing?

67
00:04:45,300 --> 00:04:48,000
So that's exactly what the system walkthrough is designed to prove,

68
00:04:48,600 --> 00:04:50,360
and it has to hit three distinct marks.

69
00:04:50,640 --> 00:04:51,540
Okay, what's number one?

70
00:04:52,020 --> 00:04:52,440
Number one,

71
00:04:53,100 --> 00:04:56,380
an update sent from a read-only service must be refused outright.

72
00:04:56,560 --> 00:05:03,260
Right. Obviously. Number two, that exact same update, when sent from the authorized registration service, must be accepted.

73
00:05:03,680 --> 00:05:07,160
Makes sense. And the third. Number three, transparency.

74
00:05:07,820 --> 00:05:14,880
The parent's report, accessed via delegated access for their child, must successfully show that one read, who the reader was,

75
00:05:15,380 --> 00:05:16,640
and the exact time it happened.

76
00:05:17,180 --> 00:05:18,780
Even if a record gets deleted later.

77
00:05:19,140 --> 00:05:21,900
Yeah, even if it's deleted, the logical record remains.

78
00:05:22,280 --> 00:05:27,200
The actual data is gone, but the digital footprint of the access history stays completely intact.

79
00:05:27,780 --> 00:05:37,700
Wow. So to sum it all up, we're talking about one unified interface where each department acts like a bank teller, handing out only the specific fields a service is authorized to

80
00:05:37,760 --> 00:05:43,820
see. Exactly. And crucially, every parent can see exactly who accessed their child's data.

81
00:05:44,060 --> 00:05:51,440
It turns data security from this, you know, hidden back office IT function into a highly visible, manageable feature for the public.

